Upstream:      OpenShell (github.com/ai-openshell/openshell):
               crates/openshell-driver-kubernetes/src/sandbox_runtime.rs, driver.rs
Upstreamable:  yes
How:           A PR against main. Mention in the PR that a Service or policy
               selecting on a template label now matches supervisor pods too, and
               that only labels admitted by allowed_pod_labels (#4497) reach it.
               Tracked in nix-q7Q31 and nix-8bBR0.
Written:       2026-11-15, against OpenShell v0.1.8; rebased 2026-11-24 onto v0.1.10
Last checked:  2026-11-24: v0.1.10's dedicated supervisor pod still gets only
               control_labels
Remove when:   a release copies template labels onto the supervisor pod
Applied by:    packages/openshell/outputs.nix; agents/outputs.nix fails the build
               after 2026-12-01 while it is carried

Kubernetes driver: copy the sandbox template's labels onto the supervisor pod.
With the sandbox runtime, upstream puts template labels only on the workload
pod. The supervisor pod (os-supervisor-<sandbox-id>) makes every upstream
connection and alone holds the SPIFFE Workload API socket, but carries only
fixed openshell.ai labels. So a SPIRE ClusterSPIFFEID cannot build an ID from
labels the caller chose (here agents.garvey.sh/type), and no other label
selector can tell supervisors apart.

The patch reads the labels from the Sandbox resource's pod template, so the
create and restart paths agree. Template labels under openshell.ai or a
subdomain of it are dropped, and the control labels (now including the
boundary-pair labels) are applied last, so boundary-role, boundary-pair,
component and sandbox-id, which the driver's identity checks and
NetworkPolicies select on, cannot be replaced. Unit tests cover both.


